(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-pd1-exam3-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"PD1 Exam 3 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form C","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What form should the assessment take?","choices":["A simple list, because the arguments belong in the differential statements","A narrative paragraph that re-argues the case for each diagnosis in turn","A ranked table of every symptom the patient reported","A copy of the chief complaint in the patient's own words"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes the assessment as a simple list, since all arguments for the diagnosis should already have been made in the differential statements. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Why might breast sensitivity to touch be increased at certain times?","choices":["If the patient is premenstrual, breasts tend to be more sensitive to touch","Breast sensitivity to touch is entirely unrelated to the menstrual cycle or hormones","Breast sensitivity to touch never varies with timing or hormonal fluctuation","Breast sensitivity to touch only increases significantly after menopause begins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Premenstrual timing is specifically noted as a cause of increased breast sensitivity to touch. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does the lecture instruct you to document in the physical examination section?","choices":["The conclusions you reached about each body system examined","Only those findings that differ from the expected normal","What you observed","The patient's own description of how each area felt to them"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says to document what you observed, starting with the general survey and moving through all body systems. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Where is the urethral (external) meatus located relative to the clitoris?","choices":["Directly within the vaginal introitus itself, sharing a single common opening","Anterior to the clitoris, just beneath the clitoral hood and prepuce","Lateral to the labia majora, near the inguinal skin folds","Posterior to the clitoris, as a slightly vertical, elongated orifice"],"answer":3,"exp":"The urethral meatus is a slightly vertical, elongated orifice located posterior to the clitoris. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"What happens to the breast during the aging process?","choices":["Breasts diminish in size, glandular tissue atrophies and is replaced by adipose tissue, and ducts become easily palpable as firm strands","Breasts remain unchanged throughout life, with no atrophy of glandular tissue or ductal changes at any age","Breasts enlarge due to glandular hyperplasia and increased vascularity, similar to changes seen in pregnancy","Nodularity increases progressively while adipose tissue is replaced entirely by dense fibrous connective tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Aging causes breast size reduction, glandular atrophy replaced by adipose tissue, and palpable firm ductal strands. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"For the endocervical Pap smear specimen, how is the cotton swab used?","choices":["Rotated within the vagina only, then discarded without ever being smeared onto a slide for cytologic review","Inserted into the cervical os, rolled between fingers, and smeared onto the slide with a painting motion, then fixed","Used to collect a squamocolumnar specimen by scraping the ectocervix in a full circular motion","Rubbed externally over the ectocervix surface only, never advanced into the cervical os itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"This is the correct cotton swab technique for obtaining and preparing the endocervical specimen. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"The lecture gives a rule about abbreviations in the physical examination. What is it?","choices":["Abbreviations are preferred because they keep the note concise","Abbreviations are acceptable if defined once at the top of the note","Only abbreviations approved by the institution may be used","Avoid them — when in doubt, spell it out"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states this in the physical examination section and repeats it in the summary, warning that acronyms and abbreviations may cause misunderstandings and medical errors. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"The plantar (Babinski) response evaluates the integrity of which pathway?","choices":["The corticospinal tract","The spinothalamic tract","The posterior columns","The vestibular system"],"answer":0,"exp":"The plantar (Babinski) response, tested at the L5 and S1 levels, evaluates the integrity of the corticospinal tract. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which part of a history and physical does the lecture say the subjective section most resembles?","choices":["The history of present illness","The review of systems","The physical examination","The identifying data"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says the subjective section is similar to the history of present illness — the story of the patient's complaint and other relevant information. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which question does the lecture pose to help decide what must be examined?","choices":["Which examination will the patient tolerate most comfortably?","What must be examined to rule in or rule out the worst-case scenario?","Which examination is quickest to document in the medical record?","Which findings are most likely to be normal on this particular patient?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs this with asking what your differentials are and how you can rule them in or out. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why should the patient's arms never be positioned over her head during the pelvic exam?","choices":["It increases the risk of vasovagal syncope","This position tenses the abdominal muscles","It is uncomfortable but has no effect on the exam","It obstructs the examiner's view of the perineum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Arms overhead tense the abdominal muscles, which is counterproductive to the relaxation needed for the exam. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which manoeuvres does the sample note say increase the patient's pain to 7 out of 10?","choices":["Lying flat, coughing, and holding her breath","Standing up quickly, walking, and climbing stairs","Turning her head, swallowing, and speaking","Leaning her head down, smiling, and chewing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The note names these three as raising the pain to 7 out of 10. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What technique brings the cervix into full view once the speculum is opened?","choices":["Asking the patient to cough","Applying suprapubic pressure","Withdrawing the speculum completely and reinserting","\"Cupping\" of the cervix"],"answer":3,"exp":"Opening the speculum and \"cupping\" the cervix brings it into full view for inspection. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"The anterior talofibular ligament, calcaneofibular ligament, and posterior talofibular ligament are ligaments of which region?","choices":["The medial knee","The lateral ankle","The wrist","The cervical spine"],"answer":1,"exp":"The anterior talofibular, calcaneofibular, and posterior talofibular ligaments are the lateral ankle ligaments. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"On the 0-to-5 motor strength scale, what does a grade of 5 out of 5 indicate?","choices":["Active movement only with gravity eliminated","Active movement against resistance without fatigue","No muscle contraction detected at all","A barely detectable flicker of contraction"],"answer":1,"exp":"A motor strength grade of five out of five is active movement against resistance without evident fatigue — normal strength. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"Besides drainage toward the axilla, where else does breast lymphatic drainage go?","choices":["Only to the cervical nodes","Also to the collateral (contralateral) side","Only to the inguinal nodes","Exclusively to the axilla, with no other pathway"],"answer":1,"exp":"Breast lymphatic drainage also goes to the collateral side, in addition to the axilla. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"What are rugae, and when are they typically absent?","choices":["Folds of the vaginal mucosa, absent in children and post-menopausal females","The fan-like fimbriated ends found at the distal fallopian tubes","Pigmented patches of skin scattered across the labia majora","Folds of the vaginal mucosa, absent only during active pregnancy and lactation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vaginal rugae are mucosal folds that are absent in children and post-menopausal women (states of lower estrogen). — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"What draping/exposure approach is used during the breast exam?","choices":["No draping is ever used at any point, from initial inspection through the final palpation step","The chest remains fully gowned throughout the entire exam, with the gown only briefly lifted for palpation","Only one breast is ever exposed at any point, with the other kept gowned for the entire visit","Full chest exposure for inspection; after inspection, one breast can be covered while the other is examined"],"answer":3,"exp":"Full exposure is needed for inspection, after which one breast can be draped while the other is examined. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does the timing element of OPPQRST capture, according to the lecture?","choices":["Whether the pain is intermittent or constant, and how it changes over time","The hour at which the patient first sought medical care","The interval between the onset of pain and the start of treatment","The number of days the patient has been away from work"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture expands timing as intermittent, constant, or changing over time. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What does the sample note record on palpation of the sinuses?","choices":["Both the maxillary and the frontal sinuses are tender to palpation","Only the right maxillary sinus is tender, with the left unaffected","Maxillary sinuses tender bilaterally, frontal sinuses not tender","All of the sinuses are entirely nontender to palpation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The note separates the two sinus groups in exactly this way. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How is pelvic muscular strength tested at the end of the bimanual exam?","choices":["This assessment is not part of the standard bimanual exam, and is deferred entirely to a specialized pelvic floor therapy referral scheduled as a separate visit","Measure vaginal wall thickness with a set of small calipers, comparing readings at three points along the canal, then repeating after a five-minute rest","Ask the patient to cough forcefully three times while the examining fingers remain at the cervix, checking for descent of the pelvic organs or urinary leakage","Withdraw fingers to clear the cervix, spread to touch the vaginal wall, and ask the patient to squeeze around the examining fingers, holding for a minimum of 3 seconds at full strength"],"answer":3,"exp":"This describes the correct technique for testing pelvic muscular strength during the bimanual exam. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"What does the sample note record about sinus transillumination?","choices":["Transillumination is present on both sides","Transillumination is absent on both sides","Transillumination was not attempted at this visit","Absent on the right, present on the left"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The note pairs the absent right-sided transillumination with the right-sided maxillary findings. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Before speculum insertion, what preliminary step helps identify the cervix's position?","choices":["Performing a full rectal exam first to estimate the uterine position and size before insertion","Auscultating the lower abdomen with a stethoscope to listen for bowel sounds and vascular bruits beforehand","Insertion of the index finger to identify the cervix by feel (firm, round surface), aiding the angle of speculum insertion","Percussing the lower abdomen and suprapubic region to estimate bladder fullness and organomegaly first"],"answer":2,"exp":"A preliminary index finger insertion helps locate/guesstimate the cervix position, which aids proper speculum angle. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the stated purpose of documenting pertinent negatives in the history of present illness?","choices":["They record findings that the patient could not recall clearly","They demonstrate that a full review of systems was completed","They shorten the note by replacing the review of systems entirely","They help rule out alternative diagnoses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs them with pertinent positives, which argue for a diagnosis, while negatives help rule out alternatives. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does the lecture recommend about body language during a first encounter?","choices":["Take notes continuously so nothing the patient says is missed","Remain seated behind a desk to maintain professional distance","Open posture and appropriate eye contact, with distractions avoided","Mirror the patient's posture exactly throughout the interview"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs these with making a good first impression and learning about patients rather than only their problems. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What breast/axilla inspection component precedes assessment of specific findings like color and contour?","choices":["Nipple assessment always comes before general appearance","Only the axilla, never the breast itself","General appearance of the breast and axilla","Palpation always precedes inspection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Overall appearance of the breast and axilla is inspected first, before assessing specific findings like color, thickening, size/symmetry, and contour. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which set of four areas does the lecture group under the mnemonic used to elicit the patient's perspective on their illness?","choices":["Frequency, intensity, family history, and examination findings","Feeling, ideas, function, and expectations","Findings, impression, formulation, and evaluation","Focus, insight, function, and emotional response"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines these as feeling (fears or concern), ideas (about nature and cause), function (how life has been affected), and expectations (of the illness, clinician, or health care). — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"During the optic nerve exam, what must be done before shining a light in the patient's eyes?","choices":["The gag reflex must be tested first","Visual acuity must be tested first","The reflexes must be graded first","The tongue must be inspected first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Visual acuity must be tested before shining a light in the patient's eyes, because bright light can transiently affect the acuity assessment. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"A grade of 3 out of 5 motor strength corresponds to what?","choices":["No muscle contraction detected at all","Movement against gravity and full resistance","A barely detectable trace of contraction","Active movement against gravity but not resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"A motor strength grade of three out of five is active movement against gravity but not against added resistance. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"Adding awareness of the event or circumstances of the illness to person, place, and time makes a patient oriented to what degree?","choices":["Oriented times two","Oriented times three","Oriented times six","Oriented times four"],"answer":3,"exp":"Orientation to person, place, time, and event is documented as oriented times four. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"What does the lecture say a first encounter with a patient establishes?","choices":["The legal basis on which subsequent documentation rests","The tone for further visits","The sequence in which body systems will later be examined","The reliability rating that will be recorded in the identifying data"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture opens its history-taking section by saying the first encounter sets the tone for further visits, and stresses a good first impression. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"In the lecture's sample vital signs, what detail accompanies the blood pressure reading?","choices":["The arm used and the patient's position","The cuff size and the device manufacturer","The time elapsed since the patient last stood","The reading from both arms averaged together"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The sample records blood pressure as 120/80 mm Hg right arm, seated. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which six maneuvers are used to test shoulder range of motion?","choices":["Pronation, supination, inversion, eversion, flexion, extension","Dorsiflexion, plantar flexion, radial and ulnar deviation, flexion, extension","Flexion, extension, adduction, abduction, internal rotation, external rotation","Protrusion, retraction, elevation, depression, rotation, tilt"],"answer":2,"exp":"Shoulder range of motion is tested with six maneuvers: flexion, extension, adduction, abduction, internal rotation, and external rotation. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"What position is used for static (non-dynamic) breast inspection, and with the arms in what position?","choices":["Supine, with arms overhead","Standing, with arms crossed over the chest","Sitting, with arms relaxed at the sides","Prone, with arms behind the back"],"answer":2,"exp":"Static inspection begins with the patient sitting, arms relaxed at the sides, before proceeding to dynamic maneuvers. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which set of words does the lecture offer as examples of pain quality?","choices":["Mild, moderate, severe, unbearable, intolerable","Constant, intermittent, episodic, cyclical, unremitting","Anterior, posterior, medial, lateral, superficial","Sharp, dull, crushing, burning, tearing, throbbing, aching"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists these as quality descriptors within OPPQRST. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"The sternoclavicular joint is formed by which structures?","choices":["The medial end of the clavicle with the upper sternum","The lateral clavicle with the acromion","The humeral head with the glenoid fossa","The scapular spine with the first rib"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sternoclavicular joint is formed by the medial end of the clavicle articulating with the upper sternum. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"In the musculoskeletal exam, what does the 'IPROMS' order of examination stand for?","choices":["Injection, Percussion, Reflexes, and Motor strength","Inspection, Palpation, Range of motion, Special maneuvers","Inspection, Percussion, Reflexes, Sensation","Imaging, Palpation, Reduction, Splinting"],"answer":1,"exp":"The IPROMS order of the musculoskeletal exam is Inspection, Palpation, Range of Motion, and Special maneuvers. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which distraction (reinforcement) technique can help elicit a lower-limb reflex that is hard to obtain?","choices":["Having the patient hold their breath fully","Having the patient close both eyes tightly","Having the patient clasp the hands and pull them apart","Having the patient stand on one leg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Distraction techniques such as clasping the hands and pulling against them (or clenching the jaw) can reinforce a hard-to-elicit reflex; a reflex needing reinforcement is graded one. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 58"},{"q":"Why does the lecture's closing summary say to avoid acronyms and abbreviations?","choices":["They make notes harder for billing staff to code accurately","They are prohibited by the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services","They lengthen the time required to dictate a note","They may cause misunderstandings and medical errors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives exactly this reason and repeats the rule: when in doubt, spell it out. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"In the LOCQSMAT mnemonic, what does the letter M represent?","choices":["Medications the patient takes regularly","Mechanism by which the injury occurred","Modifying factors","Maximum severity reached so far"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. LOCQSMAT runs location, onset, chronology, quality, severity, modifying factors, additional symptoms, and treatment. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the clitoral prepuce?","choices":["The periurethral glands opening on either side of the urethral meatus","The rounded folds of adipose and connective tissue extending from the mons pubis","The cutaneous covering of the clitoris, sometimes called the female foreskin","The erectile glans tissue at the tip of the clitoris, richly innervated"],"answer":2,"exp":"The prepuce is the cutaneous covering of the clitoris, analogous to the foreskin. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"At the end of the rectovaginal exam, what should be checked while withdrawing the rectal finger?","choices":["Prostate size","Nothing further is assessed at this point","Sphincter tone","Bladder distension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sphincter tone is checked while withdrawing the rectal finger at the conclusion of the rectovaginal exam. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which is listed as a common or concerning musculoskeletal symptom?","choices":["Improved energy levels after exercise","Joint pain with fever, chills, rash, or weight loss","A normal appetite with no pain","Full painless range of motion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Joint pain accompanied by systemic features such as fever, chills, rash, anorexia, weight loss, or weakness is a concerning musculoskeletal symptom. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"The vestibulocochlear nerve (cranial nerve eight) is responsible for which two functions?","choices":["Smell and taste","Vision and eye movement","Balance and hearing","Chewing and facial expression"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vestibulocochlear nerve, cranial nerve eight, is a sensory nerve responsible for balance (vestibular) and hearing (cochlear). — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"Two-point discrimination on the finger pads is considered normal at approximately what distance?","choices":["About 5 centimeters","Less than 5 millimeters","About 2 centimeters","About 10 centimeters"],"answer":1,"exp":"Two-point discrimination on the finger pads is normally less than 5 millimeters, whereas on the foot it may be up to 5 centimeters. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 77"},{"q":"What consistency descriptors are used to characterize an abnormal breast finding?","choices":["Only wet or dry","Only smooth or rough","Consistency cannot be reliably assessed on palpation","Soft, hard, or firm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Consistency of an abnormal breast finding is described using the terms soft, hard, or firm. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"The Romberg test assesses station by having the patient do what?","choices":["Walk heel-to-toe in a straight line","Stand with the feet together, first with eyes open then closed","Rapidly alternate hand movements","Touch the finger to the nose repeatedly"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Romberg test assesses station by having the patient stand with a narrow base, eyes open and then closed; excessive sway with the eyes closed suggests a position-sense problem. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 85"},{"q":"Normal elbow flexion, bending the elbow, is approximately how many degrees?","choices":["About 45 degrees","About 90 degrees","About 220 degrees","About 160 degrees"],"answer":3,"exp":"Normal elbow flexion, bending the elbow, is approximately 160 degrees, with extension at about 0 to negative 5 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 41"},{"q":"Why does the lecture tell you to avoid terms such as \"normal\" or \"appropriate\" in exam documentation?","choices":["Because you should describe findings in enough detail that another provider reaches the same conclusion","Because those particular words are reserved for use in the assessment section of the note","Because insurers will not reimburse notes containing them","Because they are abbreviations of longer clinical phrases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture calls these vague terms and conclusions, and asks for description detailed enough that other providers would come to the same conclusion. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Turning the sole of the foot inward, toward the midline, is which ankle movement?","choices":["Eversion","Inversion","Dorsiflexion","Plantar flexion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Turning the sole of the foot inward toward the midline is inversion, normally about 30 degrees; turning it outward is eversion. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which type of joint has essentially no appreciable movement?","choices":["A synovial joint","A cartilaginous joint","A spheroidal joint","A fibrous joint"],"answer":3,"exp":"A fibrous joint has no appreciable movement, its bones separated by fibrous tissue or cartilage. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which component does the lecture say the objective section always includes?","choices":["A general assessment or impression","A complete neurological examination","A rectal examination","A formal mental status score"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the objective section always includes the general assessment or impression. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why is contact with the inner thighs made before touching the genitalia?","choices":["It prepares the woman for the exam before genital contact occurs","It helps the examiner position the stirrups at the correct height","It has no specific purpose at all, it is purely incidental contact","It is required to assess bilateral femoral pulses before proceeding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Making contact with the inner thighs first prepares the patient for the upcoming genital exam, reducing surprise/tension. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"What does the sample note require alongside each differential diagnosis listed?","choices":["The estimated probability of it, expressed as a percentage","The name of the single test that would confirm it definitively","A brief statement correlating her findings with that potential diagnosis","A citation to the relevant published clinical practice guideline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The sample note marks each of the four entries with exactly this requirement. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A patient who arouses only after painful stimuli and lapses back to unresponsiveness when the stimulus stops is in what state?","choices":["Alertness","Stupor","Lethargy","Obtundation"],"answer":1,"exp":"In stupor, the patient arouses from sleep only after painful stimuli, gives slow or absent verbal responses, and lapses back into unresponsiveness when the stimulus ceases. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"The acromioclavicular joint of the shoulder is formed by which structures?","choices":["The lateral end of the clavicle with the acromion of the scapula","The head of the humerus with the glenoid fossa of the scapula","The medial clavicle with the upper sternum","The scapula with the posterior rib cage"],"answer":0,"exp":"The acromioclavicular joint is formed by the lateral end of the clavicle articulating with the acromion of the scapula. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"When taking a musculoskeletal history for an injury, which question addresses the 'mechanism of injury'?","choices":["What is your temperature right now?","What is your blood type?","How did the injury happen?","Who is your primary care provider?"],"answer":2,"exp":"The mechanism of injury asks how the injury happened, a key part of an accurate musculoskeletal history. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"What abnormal breast finding characteristics should be described regarding location and size, in addition to shape and consistency?","choices":["Location and size","Only the patient's age at discovery","Only whether the finding is bilateral","Only the finding's relationship to the menstrual cycle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Location and size, along with shape and consistency, are the characteristics used to describe an abnormal breast finding. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"What abnormal breast mass characteristics should be described, regarding shape?","choices":["Only whether the mass has visible pulsation","Only whether the mass is round or square","Round, disc-like, regular, or irregular","Shape is not assessed for breast masses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Shape descriptors for an abnormal breast mass include round, disc-like, regular, or irregular. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"For the supine palpation position, what preparation helps spread the breast evenly over the chest?","choices":["No specific positioning is needed for palpation, since breast tissue spreads evenly on its own","Having the patient lie flat on her back with both arms relaxed flat at her sides throughout the exam","Having the patient remain sitting upright with her arms crossed throughout the palpation","Placing a pillow under the shoulder of the examining side and having the patient rest her arm under/over her head"],"answer":3,"exp":"A pillow under the ipsilateral shoulder plus the arm positioned over/under the head spreads the breast evenly, easing nodule detection. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 32"}]};
